<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Balearic Islands</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Balearic</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Balearic Islands are an archipelago in the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mallorca">Majorca</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menorca">Minorca</a> and Ibiza. Ibiza was a Phoenician and later Carthaginian colony, and the islands were part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punic-empire">Punic Empire</a>'s network. Balearic slingers were famous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> who served in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>'s armies.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/balearic-islands.jpg" alt="Balearic Islands" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Balearic_Islands.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The archipelago lies in the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> off the eastern coast of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberian Peninsula</a>. It has two groups: the western Pityusic Islands, comprising Ibiza, Formentera and smaller islets, and the eastern Gymnesian Islands, principally <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mallorca">Mallorca</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menorca">Menorca</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> called the islands Gymnesiai, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthaginians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> used the name Baleares. Most ancient writers derived the name of the people from the Greek word for &quot;to launch&quot;, from their skill as slingers. The earliest known habitation dates to about 2500 to 2300 BCE, by people associated with the Bell Beaker culture.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In ancient times the islanders of the Gymnesian Islands built talayots and were known for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sling">sling</a>. They served as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> first under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthaginians</a> and later under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>, each man carrying three slings of different lengths for stones of different sizes. Greek and Roman writers also told stories about their customs that the Romans found peculiar. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenicians">Phoenicians</a> took possession of the islands early, and a trace survives in the town of Mago, now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahon">Mahón</a>. Quintus Caecilius Metellus conquered the islands in 123 BCE, was given the surname Balearicus, settled 3,000 Roman and Hispanic colonists and founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palma">Palma</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pollentia">Pollentia</a>.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vandals">Vandals</a> under Genseric took them between 461 and 468, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belisarius">Belisarius</a>'s troops restored Roman control in late 533 or early 534. In 707 the islands submitted to an Umayyad fleet, and in 903 Muslim forces captured them for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate-of-cordoba">Emirate of Córdoba</a>.</p><p>A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisa">Pisa</a>, with 420 ships, sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palma">Palma</a> in 1115 and withdrew, and within a year the Berber Almoravids conquered the islands. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-i-of-aragon">James I of Aragon</a> captured Palma on the last day of 1229 after a three-month siege, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menorca">Menorca</a> in 1232 and Ibiza in 1235. His will of 1276 created a Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mallorca">Mallorca</a> for his son James II, as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of Aragon.</p><p>The kingdom ended when James III was killed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-llucmajor">Battle of Llucmajor</a> in 1349 and the islands passed directly to the Crown of Aragon. Ottoman privateers raided them in the early 16th century, and in 1558 the corsair <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turgut-reis">Turgut Reis</a> ravaged them and 4,000 people were enslaved. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menorca">Menorca</a> was British for most of the 18th century after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-utrecht">Treaty of Utrecht</a> of 1713, and returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-amiens">Treaty of Amiens</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The islands form an autonomous community and province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palma">Palma</a> as capital and largest city. They achieved self-government under a Statute of Autonomy in 1983, and Catalan and Spanish are co-official languages. They have 1,249,844 inhabitants and an area of 4,991.70 square kilometres. The four principal islands are major international tourist destinations.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Balearic Islands” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The conquests of James I spread the Catalan language and settlers to Valencia and the <b>Balearic Islands</b>, and the Catalan dialect of Alghero in Sardinia survives today.</blockquote><a href="/empire/crown-of-aragon">Crown of Aragon · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Principality of Catalonia was the commercial and maritime engine of the Crown of Aragon, whose rule spread through the Mediterranean from the <b>Balearics</b> to Sicily, Sardinia, Naples and, briefly, Athens.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-catalonia">Principality of Catalonia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Carthage, until then a trading city, extended its authority over neighbouring Libyan populations, established hegemony over Sardinia, controlled western Sicily and secured the <b>Balearic Islands</b>, a source of mercenaries famous throughout the ancient world for their skill with the sling.</blockquote><a href="/empire/punic-empire">Punic Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The regency’s power rested on two corps: the ojaq of Janissaries, which garrisoned Algiers and the provincial towns and collected taxes with armed columns, and the corsair fleet, whose galleys and later sailing ships raided from the <b>Balearic Islands</b> to the Atlantic.</blockquote><a href="/empire/regency-of-algiers">Regency of Algiers · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Gaiseric conquered Sicily, Sardinia, Corsica, Malta and the <b>Balearic Islands</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/vandal-kingdom">Vandal Kingdom · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>It travelled with the Phoenician colonies along the coasts of the south-western Mediterranean, in present-day Tunisia, Morocco, Libya and Algeria, and to Malta, the west of Sicily, south-west Sardinia, the <b>Balearic Islands</b> and southernmost Spain.</blockquote><a href="/language/phoenician">Phoenician · Speakers &amp; Spread</a></li></ul></section>
